                <h2>3 Things You Must Do Before Filing an Appeal</h2>
<p>After a suspension, many people click "Appeal" immediately—and get no response. Before writing that appeal letter, complete these steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Identify the violation type</strong>: Go to Policy Status in Google Ads后台, confirm which policy was violated—counterfeit products, circumvention, inappropriate content, or something else. Different violations require different appeal materials.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Screenshot everything</strong>: The suspension notice, ad creatives, landing page screenshots, backend performance data. You may need these again after submitting.</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Pause related campaigns</strong>: If similar ads are still running in your account, pause them immediately to avoid adding new violations.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: In your backend, go to "Policy manager" and click on specific violations to see Google's explanations and policy links. This is your "reference answer" for preparing materials.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>The Core Appeal Materials Checklist</h2>
<h3>1. Appeal Form Key Points</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Best Practice</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Reason for appeal</td>
<td>Don't write "I don't think we violated anything." Clearly state that you understand which policy was violated and what corrective actions you've taken.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Remediation steps</td>
<td>Be specific: "Removed all images containing brand logos," "Changed the landing page domain"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Attachments</td>
<td>Must match the violation type—don't upload everything at once</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>2. Required Attachments</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Corrected ad creatives</strong>: Mark the differences from the original versions</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Landing page URL and screenshots</strong>: Prove the page has been updated and is compliant</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Business license or company documentation</strong>: Especially for e-commerce, gaming, and finance accounts</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Authorization documents</strong>: If running ads involving third-party brands or IP, provide authorization letters</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Appeal Letter Structure Template</h3>
<pre><code>Paragraph 1: Introduce yourself (Account ID, company name)
Paragraph 2: Acknowledge the issue (We understand we violated Policy X, Section Y)
Paragraph 3: Explain the context (misunderstanding or operational error—be sincere)
Paragraph 4: Show remediation (specific content deleted/modified)
Paragraph 5: Request review (Thank the review team, request account reinstatement)
</code></pre>
<h2>3 Real Appeal Case Studies</h2>
<p><strong>Case 1: Utility App Suspended for "Misleading Claims"</strong></p>
<p>A utility app was flagged because the ad copy said "one-click phone cleanup," which Google deemed misleading. When appealing, the team provided a product demo video and privacy policy proving the feature was real. Approved on the second appeal.</p>
<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: First appeal only said "We promise no misleading claims" without supporting evidence.</p>
<p><strong>Case 2: Cross-border E-commerce Site Wrongly Flagged for "Counterfeit Products"</strong></p>
<p>The team sold their own brand of athletic shoes but used category keywords containing a major sports brand name, triggering a counterfeit flag. They submitted trademark certificates and product photos during appeal. First appeal rejected; second appeal succeeded after adding their brand website link and domain Whois information.</p>
<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: Brand authorization documents weren't uploaded from the start.</p>
<p><strong>Case 3: Gaming Ads Suspended for "Inappropriate Content"</strong></p>
<p>A mobile game was flagged for minor violence in ad creatives. The appeal didn't acknowledge the issue—instead arguing "market compliance" and "ESRB ratings." Rejected. Second appeal deleted the controversial creatives and provided corrected versions. Account reinstated in 3 days.</p>
<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: Trying to "argue" rather than "remediate" is a common mistake.</p>
<h2>How to Follow Up After Submission</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Review timeline</strong>: Typically 3-5 business days, longer during holidays</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Don't resubmit repeatedly</strong>: Duplicate appeals for the same violation are automatically delayed by the system</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>If rejected and re-appealing</strong>: Wait at least 7 days and provide new remediation evidence</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Contact support</strong>: If no response after 10 business days, query via official Google Ads support channels</li>
</ul>
<h2>Quick Checklist: Is Your Appeal Ready?</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Have you pinpointed the specific policy clause violated?</li>
<li>[ ] Does your appeal letter address each violation point-by-point?</li>
<li>[ ] Are attachments directly related to the violation type?</li>
<li>[ ] Have you provided verifiable remediation proof?</li>
<li>[ ] Is the language concise, professional, and free of emotional language?</li>
</ul>
<p>Once you've checked all these items, your appeal materials are ready. There's no 100% guarantee for account reinstatement, but the more thorough your preparation, the higher your chances of success.</p>
